public int minTime(int n, int[][] edges, boolean[] hasApple) {
    // Initialize the distance and predecessor arrays
    int[] distance = new int[n];
    int[] predecessor = new int[n];

    // Set the distance of node 0 to 0 and its predecessor to -1
    distance[0] = 0;
    predecessor[0] = -1;

    // Loop through each node and compute the shortest distance to all other nodes
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        int minDistance = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int minPredecessor = -1;

        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (hasApple[j]) {
                int newDistance = distance[j] + 1;
                if (newDistance < minDistance) {
                    minDistance = newDistance;
                    minPredecessor = j;
                }
            }
        }

        distance[i] = minDistance;
        predecessor[i] = minPredecessor;
    }

    // Collect the apples and return to node 0
    int time = 0;
    int currentNode = 0;

    while (currentNode != -1) {
        time++;
        int nextNode = predecessor[currentNode];
        currentNode = nextNode;
    }

    return time;
}
```
This code implements the Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithm to find the shortest path from node 0 to all other nodes in the tree. It uses the distance and predecessor arrays to keep track of the shortest distance and the previous node along the path, respectively, and to collect the apples. The time required to collect all the apples and return to node 0 is returned at the end.

